          %p
            This is (
            %a{:href => "#Varadarajan04"}> Varadarajan 04, theorem 6.5.10
            ).
          %p
            For more see (
            %a{:href => "#Varadarajan04"}> Varadarajan 04, section 6.7
            ).
          %h5#PairingToVectorByChargeConjugationMatrix Pairing to a vector in terms of the charge conjugation matrix
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              In terms of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrix
              representation with respect to a chosen
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
              as in remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ChargeConjugationMatrix"}
              the pairing of prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BispinorialPairingToAVectorOverTheReals"}
              is given by the matrices
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi a
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma^a = \{(\Gamma^a)^\alpha{}_\beta\}
              that represent the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"} Clifford algebra
              by raising and lowering indices with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge+conjugation+matrix"} charge conjugation matrix
              of remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ChargeConjugationMatrix"}
              (e.g
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Freed99"} Freed 99 (3.5)
            %p
              In such a notation if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi = (\phi^\alpha)
              denotes the component-vector of a spinor, then the result of “lowering its index” is given by acting with the metric in form of the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charge+conjugation+matrix"} charge conjugation matrix
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi T
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\overline{\phi} \coloneqq \phi^T C
            %p hence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo ¯
                      %mi α
                    %mo ≔
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi β
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi β
                        %mi α
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\overline{\phi}_\alpha \coloneqq \phi^\beta C_{\beta \alpha}
                    \\,.
            %p This yields the component formula for the pairings to scalars and to vectors which is traditional in the physics literature as follows:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msup
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mi α
                            %msub
                              %mi C
                              %mrow
                                %mi α
                                %mi β
                            %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                            %msup
                              %mi ψ
                              %mi β
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mover
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mo ¯
                              %mi α
                            %msup
                              %mi ψ
                              %mi α
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mover
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mo ¯
                            %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    C(\phi,\psi) &amp;= \phi^\alpha C_{\alpha \beta} \,\psi^\beta
                    \\\
                    \&amp; =
                    \\overline{\phi}_\alpha \psi^\alpha
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \overline{\phi} \psi
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi Γ
                              %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo ,
                            %mi ψ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msup
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mi α
                            %msup
                              %mi Γ
                              %mi a
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mrow
                                %mi α
                                %mi β
                            %msup
                              %mi ψ
                              %mi β
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msup
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mi α
                            %msub
                              %mi C
                              %mrow
                                %mi α
                                %mi κ
                            %msup
                              %mi Γ
                              %mrow
                                %mi a
                                %mi κ
                            %msub
                              %mrow
                              %mi β
                            %msup
                              %mi ψ
                              %mi β
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %msup
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mi T
                            %mi C
                            %msup
                              %mi Γ
                              %mi a
                            %mi ψ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≔
                            %mover
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mo ¯
                            %msup
                              %mi Γ
                              %mi a
                            %mi ψ
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    \\Gamma^a(\phi, \psi)
                    \&amp;=
                    \\phi^\alpha \Gamma^a{}_{\alpha \beta} \psi^\beta
                    \\\
                    \&amp;= \phi^\alpha C_{\alpha \kappa} \Gamma^{a \kappa}{}_\beta \psi^\beta
                    \\\
                    \&amp; = \phi^T C \Gamma^a \psi
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \coloneqq
                    \\overline{\phi} \Gamma^a \psi
                    \\end{aligned}
                    \\,.
            %p
              (Recall that all this is here for
              = succeed "," do
                %em Majorana spinors
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BispinorialPairingToAVectorOverTheReals"}
          %p
            This yields the component expressions for the bilinear pairings as familiar from the physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            literature, for instance (
            %a{:href => "#Polchinski01"}> Polchinski 01, (B.2.1), (B.5.1)
            )
          %h5#CountingNumbersOfSupersymmetries Counting numbers of supersymmetries
          %p
            A spinor bilinear pairing to a vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \;\colon\; S \otimes S \to V
            as above serves as the odd-odd bracket in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebra"} super Poincaré Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+extension"} extension
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Lie+algebra"} super Lie algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %em supersymmetry generators
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            into minimal/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
            is also called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em number of supersymmetries
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            and in even dimensions and over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            it is traditional to write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  N = (N_+, N_-)
          %p
            to indicate that there are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_+
            copies of the irreducible
            = succeed "-representation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(V)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_-
            of those of the other chirality (i.e. left and right handed
            = succeed ")." do
              %em Weyl spinors
          %p This counting however is more subtle over the real numbers (Majorana spinors) and the notation in this case (which happens to be the more important case) is not entirely consistent through the literature.
          %p
            There is no issue in those dimensions in which the complex Weyl representation already admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+structure"} real structure
            itself, hence when there are
            = succeed "." do
              %em Majorana-Weyl spinors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_+
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_-
            as in the case over the complex numbers.
          %p
            However, in some dimensions it is only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of two Weyl spinor representations which carries a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+structure"} real structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 4
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 8
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 8
            in Lorentzian signature (see the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#RealIrreducibleSpinRepresentationInLorentzSignature"} above table
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{2} \oplus \mathbf{2}'
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 16
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 16
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{16} \oplus \mathbf{16}'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N = 1
            supersymmetry in terms Majorana spinors, even though its complexification would be
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N  = (1,1)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Freed"} Freed 99, p. 53
          %p
            Similarly in dimensions 5,6 and 7 mod 8, the minimal real representation is obatained from tensoring the complex spinors with the complex 2-dimensional canonical quaternionic representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            (as in the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#RealIrreducibleSpinRepresentationInLorentzSignature"} above table
            %em symplectic Majorana
            representations. For instance in in 6d one typically speaks of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            to refer to that with a single “symplectic Majorana-Weyl” supersymmetry (e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#FigueroaOFarrill"} Figueroa-OFarrill, p. 9
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,0)
            real supersymmetric, but which involves two complex irreps and is hence often denoted counted as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2,0)
          %h4#__superconformal_bracket
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p = 2
            – superconformal bracket
          %p
            For the moment see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "supersymmetry#ClassificationSuperconformal"} supersymmetry – Superconformal and super anti de Sitter symmetry
          %h3#ExpressionInTermsOfNormedDivisionAlgebras Expression of real representations via real normed division algebras
          %p
            We discuss a close relation between
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+spin+representations+and+division+algebras"} real spin representations and division algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#KugoTownsend82"} Kugo-Townsend 82
            %a{:href => "#Sudbery84"} Sudbery 84
            and others: The real spinor representations in dimensions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 4
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 6
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 10
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3,4,6, 10
            happen to have a particularly simple expression in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+matrices"} Hermitian matrices
            over the four real
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebras"} normed division algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            themselves, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℍ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 4
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 5
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 7
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 11
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4,5,7,11
            have a fairly simple corresponding expression. We follow the streamlined discussion in
            %a{:href => "#BaezHuerta09"} Baez-Huerta 09
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BaezHuerta10"} Baez-Huerta 10
          %h4#real_normed_division_algebras Real normed division algebras
          %p To amplify the following pattern and to fix our notation for algebra generators, recall these definitions:
          #TheComplexNumbers.num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative algebra
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              which is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generated
              from one generators
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{e_1\}
              subject to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} relation
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mn 1
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e_1)^2 = -1
                \.
          #TheQuaternions.num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℍ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              which is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generated
              from three generators
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{e_1, e_2, e_3\}
              subject to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} relations
            %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 20px 10px 20px;"}
              %img{:alt => "quaternion multiplication table", :src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/QuaternionMultiplicationTable.jpg", :width => "300"}/
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  for all
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi e
                          %mi i
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 2
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (e_i)^2 = -1
              %li
                %p
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (i,j,k)
                  a cyclic
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation"} permutation
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1,2,3)
                  then
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi e
                              %mi i
                            %msub
                              %mi e
                              %mi j
                            %mo =
                            %msub
                              %mi e
                              %mi k
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_i e_j  = e_k
                  %li
                    %p
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi e
                              %mi j
                            %msub
                              %mi e
                              %mi i
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %msub
                              %mi e
                              %mi k
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_j e_i  = -e_k
            %blockquote
              %p
                (graphics grabbed from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Baez02"} Baez 02
          #TheOctonions.num_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              The
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}
              is the
